==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|magnetise}}

===Etymology===
{{suffix|en|magnet|ize}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|magnetiz}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|physics}} To make [[magnetic]].
# {{lb|en|intransitive|physics}} To become [[magnetic]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[hypnotize]] using [[mesmerism]].
#* {{circa|1789}} {{w|Elizabeth Inchbald}}, ''Animal Magnetism: A Farce'', Dublin, P. Byron, Act III, p. 82,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004834158.0001.000]</sup>
#*: Lisette let him alone, it is dangerous to push the poor creature to extremities, Doctor, suppose we '''Magnetize''' him?
#* '''1864''', {{w|Robert Dale Owen}}, “The Convulsionists of St. Médard” in ''{{w|The Atlantic|Atlantic Monthly}}'', Volume 13, p. 347,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/atlantic13bostuoft]</sup>
#*: Dr. Bertrand tells us that the first patient he ever '''magnetized''', being attacked by a disease of an hysterical character, became subject to convulsions of so long duration and so violent in character, that he had never, in all his practice, seen the like {{...}}
# {{lb|en|figurative|transitive}} To [[attract]], [[allure]] or [[entice]]; to [[captivate]] or [[entrance]].
#* '''1861''', {{w|Oliver Wendell Holmes, Sr.}}, ''{{w|Elsie Venner}}'', Chapter 21,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/files/2696/2696-h/2696-h.htm]</sup>
#*: As for Dudley Venner, no beauty in all the world could have so soothed and '''magnetized''' him as the very repose and subdued gentleness which the Widow had thought would make the best possible background for her own more salient and effective attractions.
#* '''1894''', {{w|Bret Harte}}, “A Protégée of Jack Hamlin’s” in ''A Protégée of Jack Hamlin’s and Other Stories'', Boston: Houghton Mifflin, p. 49,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/protgeofjack00hartrich]</sup>
#*: Mr. Hamlin’s hand passed carressingly twice or thrice along her sleeve with a peculiar gentleness that seemed to '''magnetize''' her.
#* '''1982''', {{w|Richard Corliss}}, “{{w|Richard Pryor}}’s Back? Twice as Funny,” ''{{w|Time (magazine)|Time}}'', 29 March, 1982,<sup>[http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,953434,00.html]</sup>
#*: Drawing his material from the black hole of ghetto life and death, Pryor uses his dramatic power to '''magnetize''' his listeners into the fire-flash fear of the moment—even as his skewed comic perspective offers distance, safety, reassurance.

